<svelte:head>
  <title>Getting started — tsuya</title>
</svelte:head>

# Getting started

## Svelte 5

Install `@tsuya/svelte` and `@tsuya/core`. The components are in the first; the CSS, the fonts, the labels and the
colour engine they style and read are in the second, which is why both:

```bash
npm install @tsuya/svelte @tsuya/core
```

The quick start below is for SvelteKit: an app bar, a screen, a dark-mode switch that is remembered and does not
flash, and a page. Four files.

`src/routes/+layout.svelte` imports the CSS once, wraps the app in `TsuyaProvider`, and holds the screen and its bar:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import '@tsuya/core/tsuya.css';
  import '@tsuya/core/fonts.css';
  import { labelsEn, themeScript } from '@tsuya/core';
  import { Screen, SegmentedControl, TsuyaProvider, TopBar } from '@tsuya/svelte';
  import { onMount, type Snippet } from 'svelte';
  import { lit } from '../lib/lit.svelte';

  type Mode = 'system' | 'light' | 'dark';

  let { children }: { children: Snippet } = $props();

  // the user's choice, kept in localStorage; themeScript() reads the same key before the first paint
  let mode = $state('system');
  onMount(() => {
    const saved = localStorage.getItem('tsuya-mode');
    if (saved === 'light' || saved === 'dark') mode = saved;
  });
</script>

<svelte:head>
  <!-- eslint-disable-next-line svelte/no-at-html-tags -- a constant string from @tsuya/core -->
  {@html `<script>${themeScript('tsuya-mode')}<\/script>`}
</svelte:head>

<TsuyaProvider mode={mode as Mode} labels={labelsEn}>
  <Screen ambient={lit.src} ambientOpacity={lit.opacity}>
    <TopBar links={[{ href: '/', label: 'Library', current: true }]}>
      {#snippet start()}<strong>My app</strong>{/snippet}
      {#snippet end()}
        <SegmentedControl
          label="Mode"
          bind:value={mode}
          onchange={() => localStorage.setItem('tsuya-mode', mode)}
          options={[
            { value: 'system', label: 'System' },
            { value: 'light', label: 'Light' },
            { value: 'dark', label: 'Dark' },
          ]}
        />
      {/snippet}
    </TopBar>
    <main>{@render children()}</main>
  </Screen>
</TsuyaProvider>

<style>
  main {
    padding-block-start: var(--t-space-24);
  }
</style>
```

`@tsuya/core/tsuya.css` is the tokens, the base, the material and the components' styles; `fonts.css` serves Inter and
Geist Mono; `labelsEn` (or `labelsFr`, or your own `TsuyaLabels`) gives every component its system strings — close,
loading, copy and the rest — since no component holds text of its own.

`src/lib/lit.svelte.ts` holds the image that lights the screen, shared by the layout and the pages (see the accent
below):

```ts
export const lit = $state<{ src: string | null; opacity?: { light: number; dark: number } }>({ src: null });
```

`src/routes/+page.svelte` is a page, with a `Button`:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { Button, PageHeader } from '@tsuya/svelte';
</script>

<PageHeader title="Library" subtitle="4 albums" />
<Button variant="primary">Create</Button>
```

### Screen and TopBar

`Screen` is the shell of a page: the surface, the ambient background that its `ambient` image lights (a soft glow
without one), the film grain, and the content within the side margins and the safe areas. `TopBar` is a glass bar
floating 12 px from the top, sticky: put it first inside `Screen`, so that the light runs under it and the page does
not gain a bar's height of scroll. DESIGN.md does not say where the content starts below the bar, so that is the
layout's: a title right after the bar touches it, hence the `padding-block-start` on `main`.

### Dark mode without a flash

The tokens follow the system's mode. `TsuyaProvider mode` forces one, and the layout above lets the user choose and
remembers it. A page's background belongs to `<html>`, which no component reaches before JavaScript runs, so the first
paint needs help:

- for a mode that is always the same, write it on the tag in `src/app.html`, and give `TsuyaProvider` the same
  `mode`: `<html lang="en" data-theme="dark">`;
- for a choice the user makes, `themeScript(key)` of `@tsuya/core` returns a few dozen bytes of JavaScript that sets
  `data-theme` on `<html>` from `localStorage[key]` (`light` or `dark`, anything else is the system's). The layout puts
  it in the head and writes the same key when the user chooses. With a content security policy, give it your nonce.

### An accent from an image

An accent is a pair of colours, one per mode, computed from the content. `accentFromImage` of `@tsuya/core` reads a
loaded image; compute it once, in the browser as here or at upload time with `accentFromPixels`, and store it with the
content. `AccentRegion` gives its pair to everything inside it, and `Screen` takes the image as `ambient`, at the
opacity the pair computed (`ambientOpacity`), so that text on it stays readable. Replace the page with this one, and
put an image at `static/cover.jpg`:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { accentFromImage, type ImageAccent } from '@tsuya/core';
  import { AccentRegion, Button, PageHeader } from '@tsuya/svelte';
  import { onMount } from 'svelte';
  import { lit } from '../lib/lit.svelte';

  const cover = '/cover.jpg';
  let pair = $state<ImageAccent | null>(null);

  onMount(() => {
    const image = new Image();
    image.src = cover;
    image.decode().then(() => {
      pair = accentFromImage(image);
      lit.src = cover;
      lit.opacity = pair.ambient;
    });
    return () => {
      lit.src = null;
    };
  });
</script>

<PageHeader title="Library" subtitle="4 albums" />
<AccentRegion {pair}>
  <Button variant="primary">Create</Button>
</AccentRegion>
```

The rest of the API is in the components' pages, and `DESIGN.md` is the contract.

## Without a framework

`@tsuya/core` alone serves a site without a framework: `tsuya.css` holds every component's styles, and
`@tsuya/core/primitives` the behaviours that the Svelte components are built on.

```bash
npm install @tsuya/core
```

With a bundler (Vite, esbuild…), import the CSS once, as in Svelte:

```js
import '@tsuya/core/tsuya.css';
import '@tsuya/core/fonts.css';
```

Without one, serve the package's files and link them: `src/css/tsuya.css`, which imports its parts by relative URLs,
`fonts/fonts.css`, and the modules in `dist/`, named by an import map.

### The markup

Under the code of every example on a component page, **HTML** unfolds the markup that the example renders:
`tsuya.css` styles it as it is (a class without the `t-` prefix is the example's own layout). Keep its classes and
its attributes, which the styles read too (`aria-checked`, `aria-invalid`, `disabled`…), and write its text yourself,
the system strings included ("Close", "Loading"), which `labelsEn` gives the Svelte components. It is the markup at
rest, as the server renders it: what the components set in the browser (the roving tab stop, `aria-expanded`, a
dialog's `aria-describedby`, a select's chosen label) is your script's.

### The behaviour

A native control works as it is: a button, a checkbox, a radio, a switch, a text field. Whatever opens, closes, moves
focus or follows a value (a dialog, a sheet, a menu, a select, a combobox, a popover, a tooltip, tabs, a toast, a
slider's fill) is your script's, built with the primitives that the Svelte components use: each takes an element and
returns the function that detaches it. A dialog, with `modal`, which shows it in the top layer, keeps Tab inside,
gives focus back when it closes, and asks to close on Escape or a press on the scrim:

```html
<button type="button" class="t-button t-pressable t-button-secondary t-button-md" id="delete">
  <span class="t-button-label">Delete photo</span>
</button>
<dialog class="t-dialog t-dialog-md" id="delete-dialog" aria-labelledby="delete-title" aria-describedby="delete-text">
  <h2 class="t-dialog-title" id="delete-title">Delete photo?</h2>
  <p class="t-dialog-description" id="delete-text">It leaves every album it is in. This cannot be undone.</p>
  <div class="t-dialog-actions">
    <button type="button" class="t-button t-pressable t-button-secondary t-button-md" data-close autofocus>
      <span class="t-button-label">Cancel</span>
    </button>
    <button type="button" class="t-button t-pressable t-button-danger t-button-md" data-close>
      <span class="t-button-label">Delete</span>
    </button>
  </div>
</dialog>

<script type="module">
  import { modal } from '@tsuya/core/primitives';

  const dialog = document.getElementById('delete-dialog');
  let detach = null;
  function close() {
    detach?.();
    detach = null;
  }
  document.getElementById('delete').addEventListener('click', () => {
    detach = modal(close)(dialog);
  });
  // the browser may close it on its own (a close request)
  dialog.addEventListener('close', close);
  for (const button of dialog.querySelectorAll('[data-close]')) button.addEventListener('click', close);
</script>
```

The Svelte components' sources, in `packages/svelte`, show how each one puts the primitives together. The mode and
the accent work as above: `data-theme` and `themeScript()` for the mode; `setAccent(element, pair)` and
`setAmbient(element, src, opacity)` of `@tsuya/core` do what `AccentRegion` and `Screen`'s `ambient` do.

## Flutter

This is the short version; the Flutter package's README (`packages/flutter/README.md`) has the longer one. Add the
package:

```bash
flutter pub add tsuya
```

Use `TsuyaApp`, a `WidgetsApp` with the theme, the labels and fade routes:

```dart
import 'package:tsuya/tsuya.dart';

void main() => runApp(
  TsuyaApp(
    labels: TsuyaLabels.en,
    home: const HomeScreen(),
  ),
);
```

Or keep your own `MaterialApp`, `CupertinoApp` or `WidgetsApp` and put `TsuyaScope` in its `builder`:

```dart
MaterialApp(
  builder: (context, child) => TsuyaScope(
    labels: TsuyaLabels.en,
    child: child!,
  ),
  home: const HomeScreen(),
)
```

Where Material is imported too, alias the import: `Switch`, `Slider`, `Tooltip`, `Dialog`, `Chip`, `Checkbox`,
`Radio` and `Badge` exist in both.

```dart
import 'package:tsuya/tsuya.dart' as t;
```

## Tell your agents

`npx tsuya init` (after `npm install @tsuya/core`) adds a line to `AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md` pointing at the installed
package's `DESIGN.md`. `dart run tsuya:init` (after `flutter pub add tsuya`) copies `DESIGN.md` into the project and
adds the same line; run it again after an upgrade to refresh the copy.

## Rules for code that uses tsuya

- Use tokens and component props, never raw values: `var(--t-space-16)`, `TsuyaSpace.s16`.
- Components hold no text of their own: every system string comes from the labels given once at the root; every
  visible label is passed by the app.
- Customise through tokens, props and your own CSS. Never copy a component's code to change it.
- Global CSS names are prefixed: `--t-` custom properties, `t-` classes, in the cascade layer `tsuya.components`, so
  any unlayered CSS of yours wins.
